Irradiation of a $\mathrm{C_{6}D_{6}}$ solution of Ti8 $(0.95\mathrm{\mM})$ ), benzhydrol ${\mathrm{Ph}}_{2}{\mathrm{CHOH}}$ , $45~\mathrm{mM}$ ) and $\mathbf{Ar}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ internal standard for $15\mathrm{min}$ resulted in darkening of the solution to blue. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum of this reaction revealed the presence of two broad new resonances at 1.41 and $1.48~\mathrm{ppm}$ , along with the presence of unreacted $\mathbf{Ti_{8}}$ (Figure 3). Integration showed $80\%$ of starting Ti8 accounted for, and of this material, $\sim43\%$ has been converted to the new species. Treatment of this solution with 1 equiv of $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArO}^{\cdot}$ caused an immediate color change from blue to pale grey. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum of this solution shows the single set of $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}$ resonances of $\mathbf{Ti_{8}}$ $85\%$ mass balance from initial mass), and resonances ascribed to $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArOH}$ ( $64\%$ yield by initial mass). These results suggest that the new resonances correspond to a photo-reduced cluster. Its reactivity with $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArO}^{.}$ suggests that the new cluster has been reduced by $1e^{-}$ and $\mathrm{1H^{+}}$ .
The observation of only two $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}$ resonances for the reduced cluster indicates that this cluster retains the effective 8-fold symmetry of the ring on the NMR timescale. This suggests that the proton is moving around the Ti8 ring rapidly on the NMR timescale. Such rapid proton movement is consistent with the proton movement during oxidation of the reduced MIL-125.
The discrepancies in mass balance in the $\mathbf{Ti_{8}}$ chemistry indicate that partial cluster degradation accompanies photo-reduction, and that some of the NMR silent $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ species can reduce $^{t}{\mathrm{Bu}}_{3}{\mathrm{ArO}}^{.}$ (see SI for UV-vis studies). Increasing the irradiation time gave rise to several additional resonances in the corresponding $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum, indicative of multiple reduction products. Similar results are noted when pivalic acid is added as a sacrificial reductant. Resonances that correspond to pivalic acid, isobutane, and isobutene implicate the intermediacy of $\mathrm{^{\prime}B u}^{\mathrm{{.}}}$ , which disproportionates to the hydrocarbon products. Thus, the carboxylate ligands of Ti8 can serve as a sacrificial reductant (photo-reduction also occurs in the absence of an added alcohol). A similar process has been reported for pivalate on single crystal $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surfaces.36 The reduced $\mathbf{T_{i8}}$ cluster reacts much more rapidly with $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArO}^{\cdot}$ than the MOF does $\leq$ seconds vs. hours). This likely reflects the requirement that $e^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ diffuse to the surface of the MOF to react with $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArO}^{.}$ .
## Conclusions
In summary, the proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) chemistry of MIL-125 has been developed. Irradiation of MIL125 with ${}^{i}\mathrm{PrOH}$ as a sacrificial reductant gives a maximum of two $(\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{H}^{+})$ per Ti8 cluster node. Subsequent oxidation by $^{t}{\mathrm{Bu}}_{3}{\mathrm{ArO}}^{.}$ occurs by PCET to give $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArOH}$ . The extent of reduction of the MOF shows that the reducing equivalents must be stored within the MOF and not only on the outer surface. Since $^{t}\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{ArO}^{\cdot}$ is too large to enter the MOF, the PCET oxidation must, however, occur at the surface, with the $e^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ diffusing from the bulk to the surface. The coupling of $e^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ in the MOF likely results from the requirement for charge balance, that it is energetically unfavorable for charges to build up inside the MOF. This study thus presents an unusually detailed look at PCET involving a MOF material. Though there are currently very few well-defined examples, we believe that PCET will prove to be a very common type of redox reactivity in MOFs and other porous materials. This work may therefore be relevant to a number of emerging MOFapplications, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and water splitting. Our results also show the utility of the simple solution NMR methods
